WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.320
- Hello everybody, I'm Maurice Samuels

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.760
and I wanna welcome you.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.260
I'm the director of the Yale program

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.850
for the Study of Antisemitism,

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.183
and I wanna welcome you here today.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.140
Before we get started, I just
want to advertise our talk

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.630
for next week, where
I'll be in conversation

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.630
with Ken Stern about
antisemitism after October 7th.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:38.013
So we'll be trying to address
the current situation.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.730
Today, though we're gonna
go backwards in time

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:46.730
to maybe a somewhat easier
time, although maybe not,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.500
but with a real expert

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.680
who's also an old friend, Bryan Cheyette.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.550
He's Professor Emeritus of
Modern Literature and Culture

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.080
at the University of Reading

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.860
and a research fellow at the
University of Southampton.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.630
Bryan has published or edited
an astonishing 11 books.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.350
Four of his early publications
were on antisemitism

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.860
as a form of what he
calls Semitic discourse.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.495
These include "Constructions of The Jew

00:01:16.495 --> 00:01:20.760
in English Literature
and Society" from 1995

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.810
and "Between Race And Culture" from 1996.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:25.920
His recent work

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.550
connects the history of
antisemitism with colonialism

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.337
and anti-Black racism in, for example,

00:01:33.337 --> 00:01:35.070
"Diasporas of the Mind",

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.056
published by Yale
University Press in 2014,

00:01:38.056 --> 00:01:41.160
and "Ghetto, A Very Short Introduction"

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.000
published by Oxford
University Press in 2020.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.280
He's currently writing
a comparative history

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.360
of testimony from slave
narratives to refugee stories.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.120
The talk that he'll give today is called

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.410
Edwardian Popular Culture
and Everyday Antisemitism,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.410
and is part of an unpublished
article on the long history

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.310
of social media and antisemitism.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.980
So before I hand it over
to Bryan, just a word about

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.753
how things will work today.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.710
We've disabled the chat function,

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:17.100
but you can ask questions
using the Q and A function

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.230
at the bottom of your screen.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.270
And then after Bryan finishes,

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.940
I will filter those questions to him.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.330
So he plans to talk for about 40 minutes

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:30.510
and then we'll have 20
minutes for discussion.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.380
So please submit your questions
using the Q and A function.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.180
And you can submit them at any time,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.988
including during the talk.

00:02:37.988 --> 00:02:41.853
So without further ado, I
will hand it over to Bryan.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.090
- Thank you so, so much,
Maury, for that very generous

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.010
and kind introduction.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.110
And it's great to be in touch again.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:56.070
The last time I spoke at Yale,
I think I flew in, had a car,

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.640
gave my talk and flew
out a day or two later.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.706
So this is much greener, much greener.

00:03:03.706 --> 00:03:07.260
And thanks so much, Inessa,
for organizing this seminar.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.604
And I'm gonna now screen share

00:03:09.604 --> 00:03:14.604
to show you all the Inessa's
absolutely fantastic poster.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.540
Okay, so you can see the poster here,

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:23.160
which I'll come back to, the
Spider Cat, the Spider Cat.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:28.160
Also just to say that
this is part of a work

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:34.420
that speaks to the long
history of antisemitism

00:03:37.729 --> 00:03:38.812
on new media.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.640
So I can actually speak
about the present day as well

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.510
in terms of my lecture.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.345
But let's, first of all,
let's start with the history

00:03:51.345 --> 00:03:53.343
and then we'll see where we end up.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.933
So "The age of trash
triumphant," you can see,

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:03.060
even though this is a
phrase, a wonderful phrase

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.040
that Henry James' narrator uses

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:10.040
in an 1894 story, "The Next Time",

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:12.210
he thought of the present day

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:15.150
as the age of trash triumphant.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.980
That is his present day, not ours,

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.293
but of course it could also apply to ours.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.600
And as we'll see though,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.920
1894, when the story was was
written, it's significant

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.580
as it was the year when the
expensive three volume novel,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:33.243
and you can see an example
here of a three volume novel,

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:38.643
Wilkie Collins's novel we can see.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.390
Oh, I must not keep touching...

00:04:45.883 --> 00:04:46.881
There.

00:04:46.881 --> 00:04:49.140
There it is, in three volumes.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.860
Now the three volume novel
was incredibly expensive.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.520
Only libraries and the very
wealthy could afford this.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.950
And it came to an end in 1894

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:07.290
when affordable one volume paperbacks

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:11.550
or yellowbacks or penny
dreadfuls were widely produced.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:12.965
This enabled bestselling novelists,

00:05:12.965 --> 00:05:16.890
and we're looking at four
prominent examples today,

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.420
including James' close
friend George Du Maurier

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.845
who with a group ended the
library edition of the novel,

00:05:22.845 --> 00:05:27.780
which prevented authors
from profiting fully

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.783
from their work.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.380
All of the authors that we're looking at

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.570
gained a mass readership of many millions,

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.370
and all had multiple films

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.430
and theatrical productions
based on their work

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:44.763
and all kinds of other spinoffs as well.

00:05:45.755 --> 00:05:49.980
Their representations of Jews
varied from, as we'll see,

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.570
antisemitism to philosemitism
and all points in between,

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.433
what I call a Semitic discourse.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.850
The novel was, in the early
years of the 20th century,

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.800
the most accessible art form,

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.175
and sold in even larger numbers in America

00:06:04.175 --> 00:06:08.400
where they were even
more cheaply produced.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.433
And you will see that this
is a transatlantic story.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.045
And I kind of regret regret
having Edwardian in the title

00:06:16.045 --> 00:06:18.720
because really what we're talking about

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.740
is the end of the 19th,
beginning of the 20th century.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.473
But in both Britain and America.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.830
The growth of bestselling fiction

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.660
not only changed the publishing industry

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.270
and the modes of book
distribution, marketing,

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.650
and authorial entrepreneurship,
but culture itself.

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.050
For the first time the novel was portable,

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.490
easily accessible,

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.760
serialized in sensationalist
newspapers, as I've said,

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.080
and promoted by literary agents
and commercial publishers.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.123
All new, the newspapers were
new, the publishers were new,

00:06:52.123 --> 00:06:55.293
the literary agents were new as well.

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.160
Now my focus will be on the mainstreaming,

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:02.550
as we now call it, of
race thinking about Jews

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:06.120
and most other minorities
in this popular literature.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.920
Is the medium the message?

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:10.710
Did this new form of cultural production

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.920
enable extreme illiberalism in the form

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.033
of exclusionary antisemitism
to reach a mass readership?

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:22.170
And yet I'll also ask
if this is just about

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.960
the popularization of race thinking?

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.060
Or is religious discourse

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:29.973
and wider social conventions
also part of the mix?

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.430
These are the kinds of questions

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.700
which I'll attempt to answer.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:38.340
So we can see the four authors
here that we're looking at,

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:42.630
George Du Maurier, Marie
Corelli, and Guy Thorne.

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:46.890
I suspect George Du Maurier's
Svengali will be the one

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.023
that is best known to you.

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:55.293
Okay, context, everyday antisemitism.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:58.860
We'll see that antisemitism has a variety

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.900
of sources in the popular novel novels,

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:02.307
which certainly includes race thinking

00:08:02.307 --> 00:08:06.145
and the rise of political
antisemitism in the 1870s,

00:08:06.145 --> 00:08:09.600
but is often framed in
religious terms as we'll see.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.430
And refers to the debates

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:14.880
concerning Jewish immigration
from Eastern Europe.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.379
Now the point I'm making here

00:08:16.379 --> 00:08:18.900
is that the linear
history of antisemitism,

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.950
which moves historically from
religion to nation to race,

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:24.510
does not apply here.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:27.420
Each discourse exists simultaneously.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:28.980
And religion, politics, and race

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:33.440
were fierce realms of disputes
concerning the value or not

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:37.380
of Jewish citizens and Jewish refugees.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:40.560
Before the first World War,
genre race thinking in the novel

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.303
was in a state of flux.

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:48.530
But as Lisa Moses Leff argues,

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:53.430
now this is on the slide, you
can see the second quote down,

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:55.435
there's a paradox here.

00:08:55.435 --> 00:08:59.700
Liberal discourses were part
of the liberal public sphere,

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:00.930
and this certainly applies

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.513
to the novels we'll be exploring.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:09.330
So I think she makes a really
important point in a book

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:12.477
called "Jews, Liberalism,
and Antisemitism",

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.500
that our anti-Semitism emerged,

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.450
political anti-Semitism emerged

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.253
in the liberal public sphere.

00:09:23.965 --> 00:09:27.900
Popular fiction was a
mass cultural phenomenon

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:31.080
which transformed the newly
educated reading public

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.633
into book buyers, as I put here,

00:09:34.727 --> 00:09:37.170
as opposed to book borrowers,

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:39.273
which they were before the 1890s.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:45.243
But bestselling authors
didn't speak with one voice.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.920
New journalism certainly included

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.160
what I've called egregious
race thinking in the context.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.360
Also, they mainstreamed this in relation

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.460
to society and culture.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:04.260
It depends whether they
were left, liberal, or not,

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:06.990
whether they spoke in one voice.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:08.540
But novelists certainly didn't.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:13.650
Even the most egregious and
conspiratorial race thinking

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.000
divided Jews into those

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:18.300
who would make desirable
and undesirable citizens.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:22.320
And this was a phrase
used in newspapers a lot,

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.870
whether Jews were deemed
rich or poor, acculturated,

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:28.530
or religiously visible,
politically left or right.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.897
The point was, are they
a good thing or not?

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:35.280
The novels we'll explore
are sometimes quite liberal

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.300
in their outlook, sometimes
gratuitously illiberal,

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:41.730
and mainly a mixture of both.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:45.270
But they were part of the
public's sphere, as Leff argues.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:46.530
We can see this when it comes

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.020
to what I call the banalization,

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:52.320
and this is the final
quote, of Christian culture.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.410
During this period, Catholics
and Protestants were in fear

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:58.080
of sometimes violent disputes,

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.540
including anti-Catholic acts of parliament

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:04.863
to outlaw the Catholic presence
in the established church.

00:11:05.895 --> 00:11:08.190
Jews, following historical president,

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.410
were abused by Catholics or Protestants

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:12.300
insofar as they were said to resemble

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.404
their religious opponents.

00:11:14.404 --> 00:11:17.010
Within Christian theology,
Jews were after all

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:20.430
thought of traditionally as
devil figures, Christ killers,

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:24.150
as well as, through their conversion,

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.373
potential redeemers of Christ.

00:11:27.424 --> 00:11:29.070
And these discourses,

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:33.076
I think especially in America,
will be quite familiar.

00:11:33.076 --> 00:11:36.690
These ambivalent beliefs at
the heart of Semitic discourse

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.900
were particularly potent
in the popular literature,

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:43.350
which we'll now explore,
starting with "Trilby"

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:46.500
by the Anglo-French author and illustrator

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.243
George Du Maurier.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:57.090
Now we can see just one

00:11:57.090 --> 00:12:02.090
of six films on the left
here called "Svengali."

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:03.930
Sometimes they were called "Svengali",

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:06.360
sometimes they were called "Trilby".

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:10.173
This one stars Marian
Marsh and John Barrymore.

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:15.783
As prosthetic noses or
Jew-face seems to be very much

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:22.323
in our thoughts among many other things,

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:26.913
we can see John Barrymore
with his prosthetic nose.

00:12:29.185 --> 00:12:32.542
Now "Trilby" was published in
a one volume edition in 1894

00:12:32.542 --> 00:12:36.240
after the lending libraries
simply could not cope

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:38.190
with the demand for the novel.

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:42.120
It eventually sold two million
copies in Europe and America

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:43.710
and inaugurated what has been called

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:45.330
the age of the bestseller

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:49.653
after 200,000 copies were
sold in its first six months.

00:12:50.748 --> 00:12:53.017
As can be seen on the slide,

00:12:53.017 --> 00:12:56.760
"Trilby" mania as it was
called took a myriad of forms

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:58.170
and gripped America initially.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:01.050
And here, as it's a fairly long quote,

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:02.550
and my son is forever telling me

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:06.277
not to put long quotes on
slides, but I'll read it.

00:13:06.277 --> 00:13:07.830
"According to one estimate,

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:11.190
there were 24 dramatic
productions of the novel

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:12.900
playing at the same time.

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:16.260
One town in Florida
had a Svengali square,"

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:18.333
and I'm curious to see
if it's still there,

00:13:18.333 --> 00:13:19.901
"And a Little Billie Lake

00:13:19.901 --> 00:13:23.910
that was named after
Trilby's doomed admirer.

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:25.860
There were countless Trilby parties,

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:28.080
concerts based on the music in the novel,

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.450
along with soaps, toothpaste,"

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:33.097
and that's literal soaps, not soap operas,

00:13:33.097 --> 00:13:37.680
"Toothpaste, shoes,
sweets, sausages, hams,

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:41.670
hearth brushes, and the
ever present felt hat,"

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:44.130
which is still of course the trilby hat,

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:45.603
part of our vocabulary.

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:49.440
The growth of popular
novels after "Trilby"

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:52.050
was characterized by literary manias

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:53.550
up until the first World War,

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:58.020
but this did not significantly
result in a moral panic

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:02.340
about the nature of mass
appeal until after the 1920s.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.770
But the other side of moral certainty,

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:07.983
as Jonathan Friedman
has contended, you know,

00:14:09.935 --> 00:14:12.285
in his wonderful book,
"The Temple of Culture",

00:14:14.916 --> 00:14:18.540
the other side of moral certainty
is the exclusion of those

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:20.550
who do not belong.

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:25.550
Rivaling "Trilby" mania, the
love of the poor female victim.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:30.360
And we can see here Svengali,

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:35.033
I think John Barrymore
trying to hypnotize Trilby

00:14:36.627 --> 00:14:41.278
and Trilby resisting his
hypnotism in that poster,

00:14:41.278 --> 00:14:44.490
or that film still.

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:48.540
But the opposite of Trilby
mania is Svengali phobia,

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.160
Svengali phobia.

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.350
And one reason for the novel's popularity

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:55.800
was precisely such
easily digested binaries,

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.330
culture in the form of music and beauty

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:01.773
versus racial exclusion.

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:08.010
Here we can see the wonderful image,

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:10.140
and I'm pretty sure it's,

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:11.820
George Du Maurier was an illustrator,

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:15.546
so it's by George Du Maurier.

00:15:15.546 --> 00:15:20.030
And we can see this
image of the Spider Cat,

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:25.023
the weirdly ungainly Spider Cat.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:30.723
And I think, though,

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:35.250
before looking a bit
more at the Spider Cat,

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:38.010
clearly a franchise waiting to happen,

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:41.190
we need to distinguish
between the text's reception

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:44.825
and the narrative complexity
of even bestselling novels.

00:15:44.825 --> 00:15:48.600
The figure of Svengali is
extraordinarily ambivalent.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:51.750
He's both a dazzling musical genius

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:54.625
and a sexually rapacious
racial interloper.

00:15:54.625 --> 00:15:58.500
And his description removes
him from any known reality.

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:00.510
In other words, Semitic discourse

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:02.760
works on the unconscious level of fantasy,

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:05.430
as we can see by the quote

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.620
on the right hand side of the screen,

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:11.370
as well as the supposedly
more rational race science,

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:15.217
which we'll look at in the second quote.

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:20.640
But first, the phantasmagoria,

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:24.067
the fantasy surrounding Svengali.

00:16:24.067 --> 00:16:26.910
"It's as if you were
a weirdly ungainly cat

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:29.070
and most unclean."

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:30.990
Of course, the idea of the Dirty Jew

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.360
and Marie Corelli, we'll see,

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.337
uses this awful phrase quite a lot.

00:16:36.337 --> 00:16:41.280
"A sticky, haunting, long,
lean, uncanny, black Spider Cat,

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:44.115
if there is such an animal
outside a bad dream."

00:16:44.115 --> 00:16:46.440
This is very much on the level of dream,

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:48.963
the unconscious, fantasy.

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:52.140
By mesmerizing Trilby,

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:56.630
known as the apotheosis
of voice and virtuosity,

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:01.500
Svengali can enrapture his
audience to the point of,

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:04.980
in the novel's words, madness.

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:09.660
Svengali, through hypnosis,
makes Trilby's voice,

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:12.300
again quoting, irresistible.

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:14.130
It forces itself on you.

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:17.583
No words, no pictures
could ever do the like.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:23.313
Both the audience and Trilby
are under Svengali's control.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.840
Trilby mania, in other words,
is both a feature of the novel

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:31.083
and anticipates its reception.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:34.800
The literary mania surrounding Trilby

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:36.540
may have been clear cut,

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:40.680
but so-called Jewish racial
difference is both rejected

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:43.440
and universalized by Du Maurier,

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:45.540
as we can see at the start of the book

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:48.120
concerning Svengali's British rival

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:51.090
for the love of Trilby, Little Billie.

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:56.090
And here's the main quote on
the screen which I'll now read.

00:17:57.480 --> 00:17:59.130
And this is a quote, as I say,

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:01.597
from right at the beginning of the novel.

00:18:01.597 --> 00:18:04.200
"And in Little Billie's
winning and handsome face,

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:07.080
there was just a faint suggestion

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:11.520
of some possibly very
remote Jewish ancestor,

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:13.710
just a tinge of that strong, sturdier,

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:18.030
repressible, indomitable, indelible blood,

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:20.880
which is not meant to be taken pure.

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:22.260
Fortunately for the world

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:25.560
and especially for ourselves,
most of us have in our veins

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.920
at least a minimum of that precious fluid,

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:31.497
whether we know it or show it or not."

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:36.130
Now the transgressive ambiguities

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:43.770
concerning the preciousness
of Svengali's Jewish blood

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:46.140
are immensely troubling, I think.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.720
Everyone has some of this Semitic blood,

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:53.130
according to the narrator, in
their veins, but not too much.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:56.280
The novel, for all its concerns
about the virtue of Trilby,

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:58.834
is on the side of impurity

00:18:58.834 --> 00:19:02.525
as indelible blood is not
meant to be taken pure.

00:19:02.525 --> 00:19:04.650
And yet the all important boundary

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:07.920
between racial and sexual
purity and impurity

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:09.573
is impossible to locate.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:14.190
What could at least a
minimum of Svengali's blood

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:15.303
possibly mean?

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:17.970
Trilby is the product of Svengali's

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:21.963
pure blooded sexual voraciousness
and artistic virtuosity,

00:19:21.963 --> 00:19:26.133
and is shaped by these unleashed desires.

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:29.910
I've seen in the novel's
stupendous reception

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:34.050
such desires are notoriously
difficult to control

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:39.033
and could both denigrate Svengali
as well as idolize Trilby.

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:46.560
Okay, our next novel.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:49.290
And here we're going
to look at one spectrum

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:53.163
of the Semitic discourse,
the philosemitic spectrum.

00:19:56.880 --> 00:19:59.880
Trilby unleashed a veritable
slew of contradictory

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:04.320
and free floating Jewish representations,

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:06.875
which soon transforms
Svengali from the exception

00:20:06.875 --> 00:20:10.443
or the exceptional figure to the rule.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:17.673
For the rest of my talk,

00:20:20.876 --> 00:20:22.197
and I'm assuming I've got time,

00:20:22.197 --> 00:20:26.310
and I want to focus on three
bestselling authors who,

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:27.930
unlike Du Maurier,

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:31.383
combined race and culture
with Christianity.

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:36.720
And as I say, religious
discourse is just as important

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:41.720
as political and national
discourses at the time

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:45.000
and in the text that I'm reading.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:47.400
Now, I'm going to start
with, as I've said,

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:49.290
the philosemitic Hall Caine,

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:51.210
or at least he was regarded as such

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:53.735
by the Chief Rabbi, Israel Zangwill,

00:20:53.735 --> 00:20:55.355
and the Jewish Maccabean Club.

00:20:55.355 --> 00:21:00.355
This was a club of professionals,
intellectuals, artists

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:01.583
who made Caine

00:21:01.583 --> 00:21:04.863
their one and only
non-Jewish honorary member.

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:07.500
And Caine spoke at the club,

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:10.830
and I'll talk about one of
his lectures in a minute.

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:14.250
Caine's novels sold
more than anyone else's,

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:17.043
with lifetime sales of about 10 million.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:21.900
His declaration of prophetic
wisdom fed into the fiction,

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:26.880
which laid claim to spiritual
quasi Christian humanism.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:29.910
And we can see an example
of this in the first quote

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:33.150
on the slide.

00:21:33.150 --> 00:21:36.030
We can also see the extent

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:38.790
with the caricature here of of Caine,

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:41.850
the extent to which
writers became celebrities,

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:45.300
their name, as they are
today with celebrity authors,

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:46.920
above the title.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:50.070
And we can see on the
right hand image here

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:55.070
the way that novels were marketed
very cleverly and subtly.

00:21:56.370 --> 00:22:00.457
So Caine's philosemitism, the first quote.

00:22:00.457 --> 00:22:02.250
"Palestine is a Jewish commonwealth

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:03.840
protected by all the powers

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:06.063
and perhaps also partly
controlled by them.

00:22:09.444 --> 00:22:11.130
And if this is not the millennium

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:13.620
the Jewish people have
looked for and prayed for,

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:16.116
it may be a step in that
general progress of the world,

00:22:16.116 --> 00:22:20.340
which is binding all parts
and all peoples together."

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:22.867
And you can see here how he moves from

00:22:22.867 --> 00:22:26.730
religious ways of thinking, millennium,

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:30.423
to humanism, binding all peoples together.

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:34.710
Many of these novels' plot
lines were based on the Bible,

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:37.140
and he was one of numerous
authors at the time

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.600
who wrote "A Life Of Christ".

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:44.130
I'm not sure there's a book
on the numbers of books

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:45.906
on the life of Christ at the time,

00:22:45.906 --> 00:22:48.780
but I think that would
be an interesting topic.

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:50.910
He was a firm supporter of Zionism,

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:52.200
as we can see from this quote.

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:55.710
And also Zangwill's Jewish territorialism.

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:59.670
And visited Jews in the Pale of Settlement

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:01.020
on behalf of Anglo (indistinct)

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:04.380
and wrote up his experiences
in "The London Times".

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:06.840
In a series of articles he
distinguished between those

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:10.080
who traveled legitimately to the West

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:14.280
and those whom he called euphemistically

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:17.760
illegitimate refugees,
that sound familiar?

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:21.633
Or quote, independent immigrants.

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:24.510
That even philosemitism distinguished

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:27.930
between desirable and undesirable Jews

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:29.820
can be seen in the second quote,

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:32.460
which contended, on the slide,

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:34.230
which contended that Jewish persecution

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:35.607
was partly in response

00:23:35.607 --> 00:23:39.543
to Jewish self-separation
or ghettoization.

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:44.947
So this is the shorter quote on the slide.

00:23:44.947 --> 00:23:47.790
"May I dare to say that
it would be well if Jews

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:50.880
came oftener into the light
and free air of the world

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:53.670
that is common to all men."

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:56.400
Here he was reflecting
contemporary accounts

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:57.600
of anti-Semitism,

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:00.933
which blamed Jews and Judaism
for their persecution.

00:24:04.942 --> 00:24:07.680
Now one reason that
Caine was so successful

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:10.170
is that he encouraged a view of his work

00:24:10.170 --> 00:24:13.650
that associated ethical
thinking with novel writing.

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:16.500
By placing his self-proclaimed
prophetic imagination

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:18.150
at the heart of his fiction,

00:24:18.150 --> 00:24:20.040
Caine replaced the uncertainties

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:22.230
concerning the established church

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:26.955
articulated in the so-called
Great Church Crisis of the time

00:24:26.955 --> 00:24:30.993
with a higher form of
imagined righteousness.

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:34.320
His Christian humanism
meant that when it came

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:36.570
to the treatments of persecuted Jews,

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:39.120
he became a firm public opponent

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:41.340
of anti-immigration legislation

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.980
on the grounds that it went
against the liberal principles

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:48.207
which made Britain, as he
put it, "Rich and free."

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:05.890
Now this quote shows the extent to which

00:25:07.620 --> 00:25:12.620
Hall Caine was well aware of
literary Jewish stereotypes.

00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:17.280
And I think the
sophistication of this quote,

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:20.410
and this is the lecture that
he gave at the Maccabean Club

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:22.657
where he talks about

00:25:22.657 --> 00:25:24.930
"The numerous and popular grotesque Jews

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:27.960
such as Fagin in Dickens' 'Oliver Twist'

00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:29.820
and Du Maurier's Svengali."

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:32.460
These are all quotes from him.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:35.460
In contrast, he welcomed
what he called a small group

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:37.530
of noble Jewish studies.

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:41.250
So he divided novels up crudely

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:43.353
into good Jews and bad Jews,

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:47.070
and the group of noble Jewish studies

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:50.250
which reflected the
sublimity of the Hebrew Bible

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:52.020
through the lens of Scott's "Ivanhoe",

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:53.370
Benjamin's Disraeli's fiction,

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:56.360
George Eliots's "Daniel Deronda".

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:58.530
And it was in this philosemitic tradition

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:00.540
that he wanted to write in

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:04.410
and wrote a novel called
"The Scapegoats" 1891,

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:07.530
which was to follow
these earlier precedents

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:11.250
and add, as he put it,
to the noble studies

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:15.300
by portraying a cultured heroic Jew,

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:16.863
a cultured heroic Jew.

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:19.593
And in this novel,

00:26:22.284 --> 00:26:27.284
he compared his cosmopolitan heroic Jew

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:32.610
with more ghettoized Jews,
Jews who rejected him.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:36.540
And also showed the process by which

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:41.540
his hero in "The Scapegoat"
eventually became heroic.

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:46.740
And that very much included
him becoming a father

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:50.400
to a daughter who he initially rejected.

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:54.720
And of course, fathers and
daughters go all the way back

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:56.973
to Shylock and his daughter.

00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:05.300
Okay, I now want to compare
Hall Caine's philosemitism

00:27:06.360 --> 00:27:09.123
with Marie Corelli's antisemitism.

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:17.310
Marie Corelli also
associated ethical thinking

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:20.100
with her novels, but she
took entirely the opposite

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:22.770
perspective of her archrival Hall Caine,

00:27:22.770 --> 00:27:27.770
and actively opposed the
immigrants of Jews into Britain

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:30.180
and was one of the most
prominent supporters

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:33.180
of the 45,000 strong
British Brothers League

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:36.690
who campaigned for
anti-immigrant legislation,

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:38.760
the first such act of its kind,

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:41.733
which was eventually passed in 1905.

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:45.990
A favorite of Queen Victoria's
and Winston Churchill's,

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:49.200
Corelli was the most popular
woman writer of her age,

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:52.620
who most notably, as I say,
rejected liberal verities

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:56.400
in the name of her
bespoke Christian values.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:59.434
Her fame at its height resulted
in her best known novels.

00:27:59.434 --> 00:28:01.470
She published 30 in all,

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:04.350
selling more than a
100,000 copies annually,

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:08.965
with overall sales close to
the 10 million of Hall Caine.

00:28:08.965 --> 00:28:10.770
Whereas Caine was on the side

00:28:10.770 --> 00:28:13.320
of tolerant British liberal conventions,

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:17.250
as seen in his opposition to
anti-immigrant legislation,

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:20.370
Corelli, his antithesis,

00:28:20.370 --> 00:28:23.220
was distinctly illiberal when it came to

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:25.860
what she called the spirit of the Jew.

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:28.800
And she talked about the spirit of the Jew

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:31.890
in an essay which I'll now read a chunk of

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:34.287
called "Society and Sunday".

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:38.970
The phrase "The spirit of the Jew"

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:43.650
I think was very much
in the air at the time.

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:45.660
In terms of Otto Weininger's,

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:48.480
the translation of "Sex and Character",

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:51.270
which was another popular text

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:54.213
which talked about the spirit of the age,

00:28:56.386 --> 00:29:00.180
meaning that modernity is Jewish.

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:03.720
And there's been a lot
of work on Weininger.

00:29:03.720 --> 00:29:05.190
I think the point that I want to make,

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:08.040
and I haven't had time
to make it in this talk,

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:09.870
is that there's all kinds of connections.

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:12.930
And I'll make a few later on.

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:14.790
I've already talked about
James and Du Maurier,

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:17.520
there's a lot more to be said about that.

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:19.413
We'll look at TS Elliot.

00:29:21.828 --> 00:29:24.360
I haven't time to look at
Joyce or Virginia Woolf

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:26.640
or Dorothy Richardson or Djuna Barnes.

00:29:26.640 --> 00:29:28.890
But the links between
high and low culture,

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:33.120
the popular and the literary,
is very much a subject

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:34.920
that needs exploring.

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:39.330
But here we just have the
gutter, Marie Corelli.

00:29:39.330 --> 00:29:42.877
And I'll read now from
"Society and Sunday".

00:29:42.877 --> 00:29:45.720
"According to the latest
views publicly expressed

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:49.200
by both Christians and un-Christian
clerics, it would appear

00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:53.460
that 20th century society
is not one with Sunday.

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:56.580
It no longer keeps the seventh day holy.

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:00.600
It reads its up to date
books and magazines

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:03.720
and says the Sabbath is
a Jewish institution.

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:06.070
And though the spirit of the Jew pervades

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:10.595
my whole constitution and composition,"

00:30:10.595 --> 00:30:13.020
and here she's speaking in the voice

00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:15.637
of the un-Christian priests,

00:30:15.637 --> 00:30:17.610
"And though I borrow money off the Jew

00:30:17.610 --> 00:30:19.470
whenever I find it convenient,

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:21.060
there's no reason why I should follow

00:30:21.060 --> 00:30:23.487
the Jews' religious ritual."

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:27.270
So we can tell her association here

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:29.343
between modernity and Jews,

00:30:31.166 --> 00:30:34.830
and she takes very much an
anti-modern stance in the name

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:37.587
of her bespoke Christianity."

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:40.710
A letter of support was
read out at the rallies

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:42.570
of the British Brothers League

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:44.250
and called for branches of the BBL

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:47.580
to counter what she called
the constant foreign invasion

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:50.190
of our land and our trade interests.

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:52.710
Although the leaders of the
BBL, which included members

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:54.870
of parliament for the east end of London,

00:30:54.870 --> 00:30:56.490
deliberately spoke of alien

00:30:56.490 --> 00:30:58.740
rather than Jewish immigrant immigrants

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:02.610
to avoid charges by the
liberal party of antisemitism,

00:31:02.610 --> 00:31:04.200
this was not the case in practice

00:31:04.200 --> 00:31:06.690
where cries of No More Jews, Sweater,

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:10.849
and Go Back To Jerusalem, were
commonplace at their rallies.

00:31:10.849 --> 00:31:13.560
Jews were also described
in east end newspapers

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:17.490
written by BBL's supporters
as quote, "A plague

00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:21.990
or exacting blood money or
crucifying East London."

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:24.937
And as generally more demonic than human.

00:31:24.937 --> 00:31:27.927
"Fancy using Christian
blood for their ritual",

00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:34.010
was one not very fanciable fancy
in the east end newspapers.

00:31:37.200 --> 00:31:41.070
Now Corelli was quite at
home with such language.

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:44.010
In her 1894 novel called "Barabbas",

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:45.870
a rewriting of the Gospels,

00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:48.270
and quite often she wrote

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:52.190
as if it was another form
of the King James Bible,

00:31:52.190 --> 00:31:55.290
she evoked the blood libel
when her character Barabbas

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:58.254
describes the crucifixion as quote,

00:31:58.254 --> 00:32:02.400
"A great feast of slaughter,
the apotheosis of the Jews."

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:04.680
This man of Narazeth was slain to satisfy

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:09.027
the bloodthirstiness of the
God-elected children of Israel."

00:32:10.050 --> 00:32:11.910
The unresolved nature of Jewish blood

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:15.720
in Du Maurier's "Trilby",
published in the same year,

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:18.243
is here settled decisively.

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:25.173
Now, Corelli's antisemitic perspective,

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:28.710
placing very much race over culture.

00:32:28.710 --> 00:32:33.450
And whereas I think the distinction I make

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.750
is whereas Corelli
places race over culture,

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:39.333
Caine places culture over race.

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:43.560
Now, her perspective I think
is expressed most succinctly

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:47.697
in the 1999 title story
called "The Song of Miriam",

00:32:48.570 --> 00:32:51.480
which concertinas together
an astonishing large number

00:32:51.480 --> 00:32:53.790
of literary portrayals of Jewish women

00:32:53.790 --> 00:32:57.000
following the all-encompassing
style of Du Maurier.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:59.340
Based in the slums of Paris,

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:02.370
the story concerns the
murder of the elderly

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:04.710
and poverty-stricken Reuben David,

00:33:04.710 --> 00:33:07.653
who lives with Miriam, his
14 year old granddaughter,

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:13.743
and is killed by his
predatory nephew Joseph Perez.

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:17.970
Miriam becomes a famous
Parisian opera singer

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:21.277
known as the Divine Miriami, who quote,

00:33:21.277 --> 00:33:25.260
"Looked like a goddess
and sang like an angel."

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:28.170
Her ravishing grace had
won her the admiration

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:31.650
of kings and emperors, we're told.

00:33:31.650 --> 00:33:34.110
Such preeminence on the Parisian stage

00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:38.130
makes her a cross between
Rachel Felix and Sarah Bernhardt

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:43.130
as well as conjuring up the
decadent Paris in "Trilby".

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:45.510
But unlike Felix and Bernhardt,

00:33:45.510 --> 00:33:49.410
Miriam stabs Reuben David
in front of her audience

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:50.243
with the knife that was used

00:33:50.243 --> 00:33:53.280
to kill her cherished grandfather.

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:56.525
She justifies her actions
by contending brazenly

00:33:56.525 --> 00:33:58.807
that he deserved his fate.

00:33:58.807 --> 00:34:02.340
"It is a just vengeance," she says.

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:05.970
Here, race and religion is
once again indistinguishable,

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:08.730
as we can see from some
more quotes from the story.

00:34:08.730 --> 00:34:11.125
And here I'll read from the slide.

00:34:11.125 --> 00:34:13.680
And we can see an image

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:17.220
of one of Marie Corelli's melodramatic,

00:34:17.220 --> 00:34:18.990
and you can hear from her style,

00:34:18.990 --> 00:34:22.380
incredibly melodramatic
stories turned into films,

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:24.660
turned into theater.

00:34:24.660 --> 00:34:26.250
Here, the new woman,

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:30.150
who also was very much
castigated by Marie Corelli,

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:33.000
is going towards the devil,

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:34.350
the Satan figure in the novel.

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:38.490
And you have a man pulling
her back in that front cover.

00:34:38.490 --> 00:34:41.760
But let's read the quote from the story

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:45.517
to get a bit more of Corellian melodrama.

00:34:45.517 --> 00:34:48.090
"A flash of large, bright, eager eyes,

00:34:48.090 --> 00:34:50.266
a glimmer of red lips and white teeth,

00:34:50.266 --> 00:34:54.120
and the tip of a decidedly Jewish nose

00:34:54.120 --> 00:34:57.020
was all that could be discerned
of the 14 year old Miriam.

00:34:57.870 --> 00:34:59.850
Joseph Perez could hardly,

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:03.150
and this is when he finds
himself on the stage

00:35:03.150 --> 00:35:07.207
with Miriam, when they're
both successful opera singers,

00:35:07.207 --> 00:35:11.250
"Joseph Perez could hardly
enunciate either words or music

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:15.270
so appalled was he at the
terrible look of Miriam's eyes."

00:35:15.270 --> 00:35:16.807
It's always the eyes.

00:35:16.807 --> 00:35:20.100
"For he saw his own dagger,
used to kill Reuben David,

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:21.990
glittering in her hands.

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:26.990
And what was far worse, he saw vengeance."

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:31.503
And of course, well,
vengeance we can come back to.

00:35:32.910 --> 00:35:37.837
Summed up where we move very
much from race to religion.

00:35:37.837 --> 00:35:41.580
"Those who follow the Mosaic
law are not bound to love

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:43.380
or forgive their enemies.

00:35:43.380 --> 00:35:48.380
And the God of the Old Testament
is not the God of the New."

00:35:52.950 --> 00:35:57.950
Okay, Guy Thorne, our
last and final novelist.

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:03.330
Here I just want to look
at Jewish conspiracies

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:04.560
in their nascent form,

00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:08.670
before "The Protocols
of the Elders of Zion".

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:13.670
And there were all kinds
of conspiracies to do with

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:17.490
financial scandals at the time,

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.190
but they were more complicated, I think.

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:23.280
It's a great mistake to
read back from the Protocols

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:26.370
after the Russian Revolution
and the First World War.

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:28.053
So Guy Thorne.

00:36:32.208 --> 00:36:34.710
Now, Corelli's shameless
race thinking encouraged

00:36:34.710 --> 00:36:38.640
a slew of outrageously
illiberal popular writers

00:36:38.640 --> 00:36:41.190
in the first two decades
of the 20th century.

00:36:41.190 --> 00:36:45.000
And I'm looking at the best
known of these popular writers.

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:48.750
There are many, many more
that are hardly known about,

00:36:48.750 --> 00:36:49.800
hardly known at all.

00:36:49.800 --> 00:36:51.000
I've got a few of them,

00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:54.630
I'm just looking at my floor covering.

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:58.650
Yes, here's one that no one
has written a word about.

00:36:58.650 --> 00:37:03.650
T Kingston Clark, T Kingston
Clark, "Men versus Devils".

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:06.593
I bet you can guess who are
the men and who are the devils.

00:37:07.590 --> 00:37:10.530
Lots and lots of these
such popular fiction.

00:37:10.530 --> 00:37:13.740
I'm looking at the four
best known, bestselling,

00:37:13.740 --> 00:37:15.393
most influential of them.

00:37:18.915 --> 00:37:20.763
The rest are largely forgotten.

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:25.290
All of these popular novels
were to employ a heady mix

00:37:25.290 --> 00:37:29.400
of immovable racial and
theological distinctions

00:37:29.400 --> 00:37:31.533
when it came to the figure of the Jew.

00:37:32.550 --> 00:37:35.280
But it would be wrong to assume

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:37.814
that they were all writing
from the same perspective,

00:37:37.814 --> 00:37:40.860
certainly in this period.

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:45.210
Guy Thorne, for instance, was
a professing Anglo-Catholic

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:48.007
whose best known work of fiction,

00:37:48.007 --> 00:37:50.940
"When It Was Dark, The Great Conspiracy"

00:37:50.940 --> 00:37:54.390
is its subtitle, was described by Freud,

00:37:54.390 --> 00:37:56.550
and we can see this from the short quote

00:37:56.550 --> 00:38:00.960
underneath the book cover on your screen,

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:02.497
was described by Freud as

00:38:02.497 --> 00:38:05.490
"An English novel of Catholic origin."

00:38:05.490 --> 00:38:10.200
And the reason that Freud
referred to the novel

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:12.930
was because he was looking
at group psychology,

00:38:12.930 --> 00:38:14.160
writing about group psychology

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:16.290
and was writing about the Catholic church.

00:38:16.290 --> 00:38:21.290
And as we'll see, "When It Was Dark"

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:24.607
was absolutely everywhere in 1903

00:38:25.710 --> 00:38:27.873
when Freud was doing his research.

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:38.940
Most bestsellers at the
time, and this is the point,

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:42.960
were militantly Protestants,
militantly anti-Catholic.

00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:47.960
And this was part of the
campaign to remove Catholicism

00:38:48.810 --> 00:38:51.300
from the established church at the time,

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:54.120
the Great Church Crisis.

00:38:54.120 --> 00:38:57.483
So Guy Thorne was writing
very much against the grain.

00:38:58.470 --> 00:39:00.270
But Thorne's pro-Catholic fiction

00:39:00.270 --> 00:39:03.810
caught the mood of the time
by denouncing what he called,

00:39:03.810 --> 00:39:06.090
and this is a quote from him.

00:39:06.090 --> 00:39:07.323
He was a journalist.

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:09.090
In his journalism

00:39:09.090 --> 00:39:12.725
he talked about the
curse of indifferentism,

00:39:12.725 --> 00:39:15.363
or what we would call today secularism,

00:39:16.290 --> 00:39:18.870
as opposed to the supreme moral

00:39:18.870 --> 00:39:22.260
and spiritual principles he championed.

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:25.147
And he certainly championed
them in his novel.

00:39:25.147 --> 00:39:27.840
"When It Was Dark" sold
half a million copies

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:30.840
and was the most read
novel I think in Britain.

00:39:30.840 --> 00:39:33.390
I'm not sure that it traveled in the way

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:38.390
that some of the other
novels traveled to America,

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:40.470
because this novel was very much part

00:39:40.470 --> 00:39:44.463
of the Great Church Crisis
in Edwardian Britain.

00:39:45.480 --> 00:39:47.400
The great conspiracy,

00:39:47.400 --> 00:39:50.790
and here we come to the great
conspiracy, is an attempt

00:39:50.790 --> 00:39:54.060
to prove that Jesus's
resurrection was a hoax

00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:57.340
by faking archeological evidence

00:39:59.130 --> 00:40:02.730
to show that the body of Christ
was removed from its tomb

00:40:02.730 --> 00:40:04.743
by Joseph of Arimathea.

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:09.000
So the resurrection was a hoax.

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:13.740
The novel's devilish
anti-hero, Constantine Schuabe,

00:40:13.740 --> 00:40:15.600
and I thought a lot about his name.

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:18.565
Constantine obviously goes
back to the Roman Emperor,

00:40:18.565 --> 00:40:21.510
but whether the Roman emperor
was anti-Christian or not

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:24.030
I think is open to debate.

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:29.030
Schuabe, as German language
speakers will know, is a robe.

00:40:31.620 --> 00:40:34.770
How that signifies, I'm not quite sure.

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:39.770
Maybe referring to the coats
that Orthodox Jews wear.

00:40:40.380 --> 00:40:42.420
We're not really sure.

00:40:42.420 --> 00:40:46.200
But Constantine Schuabe
is behind this conspiracy,

00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:49.250
and blackmails the keeper
of biblical antiquities

00:40:49.250 --> 00:40:53.790
at the British Museum to
forge the ancient records

00:40:53.790 --> 00:40:56.250
and place them in the Holy Land

00:40:56.250 --> 00:40:57.873
so that they can be discovered.

00:40:59.040 --> 00:41:03.270
Now, no wonder the Bishop of
London in Westminster Abbey,

00:41:03.270 --> 00:41:05.397
and this is the quote that
I'll read in a minute,

00:41:05.397 --> 00:41:09.555
and many of the popular preachers
sermonized on the novel,

00:41:09.555 --> 00:41:12.720
which was anticipated in the novel's plot.

00:41:12.720 --> 00:41:15.420
And now this is a familiar technique

00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:17.640
to generate a particular response.

00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:21.840
So the response of the
novel, as with "Trilby"

00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:24.720
and as with so many
Hollywood films that we watch

00:41:24.720 --> 00:41:27.270
is very much part of the plot

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:30.123
of the popular movie or popular novel.

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:33.570
So there's lots of
sermonizing in the novel,

00:41:33.570 --> 00:41:37.770
and lots of sermonizing
once the novel's published.

00:41:37.770 --> 00:41:40.600
And the most famous sermon,
which Freud refers to,

00:41:40.600 --> 00:41:44.047
is by the Bishop of London
in Westminster Abbey.

00:41:44.047 --> 00:41:47.070
"I wonder whether any of you
have read that remarkable work

00:41:47.070 --> 00:41:49.290
of fiction entitled 'When it was Dark'.

00:41:49.290 --> 00:41:51.270
It paints in wonderful colors

00:41:51.270 --> 00:41:55.878
what it seems to me the world would be

00:41:55.878 --> 00:41:57.330
if for six months,

00:41:57.330 --> 00:41:59.910
as in the story is supposed to be the case

00:41:59.910 --> 00:42:02.040
owing to a gigantic fraud,

00:42:02.040 --> 00:42:05.277
the resurrection might be
supposed never to have occurred.

00:42:05.277 --> 00:42:08.700
And you feel the darkness creep
around the world when you,"

00:42:08.700 --> 00:42:10.680
and I won't go into details here,

00:42:10.680 --> 00:42:15.680
but anarchy breaks out,
lots of women are abused,

00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:21.050
it's a real dystopia that the
novel paints, a horror show.

00:42:22.890 --> 00:42:24.750
When you see how darkness settles down

00:42:24.750 --> 00:42:26.070
upon the human spirit

00:42:26.070 --> 00:42:28.380
regarding the Christian
records as a fable,

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:31.620
then you quit with something
like adequate thanksgiving,

00:42:31.620 --> 00:42:34.110
and thank God it is light

00:42:34.110 --> 00:42:37.677
because of the awful
darkness when it was dark."

00:42:42.540 --> 00:42:45.330
Now "When It Was Dark"
has been misread, I think.

00:42:45.330 --> 00:42:47.730
And this is why I talk
about Semitic discourse,

00:42:47.730 --> 00:42:50.215
and we can talk about
this in the questions

00:42:50.215 --> 00:42:54.550
as opposed to simply
antisemitism or Jew hatred

00:42:56.040 --> 00:42:57.990
or even philosemitism.

00:42:57.990 --> 00:43:01.563
We can see that in fiction at least,

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:06.603
in fiction at least,

00:43:07.920 --> 00:43:12.920
these are quite ambivalent,
ambiguous images of Jews,

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:15.120
even in popular fiction.

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:16.200
So it's been misread

00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:19.233
as being straightforwardly
a form of Jew hatred.

00:43:24.090 --> 00:43:27.030
And it's a complicated work.

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:28.860
Unlike the other fiction, perhaps,

00:43:28.860 --> 00:43:31.620
with the notable exception
of Marie Corelli.

00:43:31.620 --> 00:43:34.650
Even Guy Thorne's nascent
Jewish conspiracy theories

00:43:34.650 --> 00:43:36.420
were bifurcated.

00:43:36.420 --> 00:43:38.520
So even though we have
Jewish conspiracy theories,

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:42.300
they were bifurcated and
made up of good and bad Jews.

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:44.040
It was only after the first World War

00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:46.860
and the Russian Revolution
that Jewish conspiracy theories

00:43:46.860 --> 00:43:50.165
were hardened into
straightforward Jew hatred.

00:43:50.165 --> 00:43:52.920
Now my point is that much
of what I'm discussing

00:43:52.920 --> 00:43:55.800
is ambivalent forms of race thinking,

00:43:55.800 --> 00:43:58.500
which I believe is akin to binary forms

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:00.120
of gender stereotyping.

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:03.300
And I'm more than happy
to expand this point

00:44:03.300 --> 00:44:05.670
in the question and answer session.

00:44:05.670 --> 00:44:08.580
As we can see from the
binary Jewish conspirators

00:44:08.580 --> 00:44:09.810
in the novel.

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:14.400
And here I think this
is the penultimate slide

00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:15.660
that I'll be looking at.

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:17.880
So I'm hoping I'm okay for time.

00:44:17.880 --> 00:44:20.823
So first of all, we're gonna
look at Constantine Schuabe,

00:44:22.927 --> 00:44:27.240
"Whose hair was a very dark
red, smooth, and abundant.

00:44:27.240 --> 00:44:28.760
His features were Semitic,

00:44:28.760 --> 00:44:30.780
but without a trait of that fullness

00:44:30.780 --> 00:44:33.810
and sometimes coarseness
which often marks the Jew

00:44:33.810 --> 00:44:36.420
who has come to the middle period of life.

00:44:36.420 --> 00:44:40.410
The eyes," we come back to the
eyes, "Were large and black.

00:44:40.410 --> 00:44:45.360
But without animation, they
were coldly, terribly aware,

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:48.960
with something of the
sinister and untroubled regard

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:52.437
one sees in a reptile's eyes."

00:44:56.249 --> 00:44:57.180
Schuabe is a man

00:44:57.180 --> 00:45:00.090
of enormous power and
influence, we're told,

00:45:00.090 --> 00:45:03.600
who owns the equivalent
of the Times newspaper,

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:05.520
had a stellar career at Oxford,

00:45:05.520 --> 00:45:07.885
and was about to enter politics.

00:45:07.885 --> 00:45:11.040
He is quote, "No longer a Jew"

00:45:11.040 --> 00:45:13.507
as he converted to Unitarianism,

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:18.780
which as the narrator
makes clear repeatedly

00:45:18.780 --> 00:45:19.710
throughout the novel,

00:45:19.710 --> 00:45:22.020
and remember, this is
written from a Catholic,

00:45:22.020 --> 00:45:23.883
a Trinitarian perspective,

00:45:24.810 --> 00:45:27.510
Unitarianism according to Guy Thorne

00:45:27.510 --> 00:45:29.703
is another form of Judaism.

00:45:30.660 --> 00:45:32.760
CS Elliot, raised as a unitarian,

00:45:32.760 --> 00:45:35.430
also believed this of his own background.

00:45:35.430 --> 00:45:38.583
And I can develop this in the
question and answer session.

00:45:39.690 --> 00:45:41.430
Schuabe, throughout the novel,

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:44.223
even though he converted,
is called The Jew.

00:45:45.180 --> 00:45:49.200
That is presumably why
he quote, "Hates Christ."

00:45:49.200 --> 00:45:53.850
And maybe Unitarianism
perhaps belittles Christ,

00:45:53.850 --> 00:45:56.967
but hating Christ, according to the novel.

00:45:56.967 --> 00:45:59.885
And he wants to undermine
faith in Christianity

00:45:59.885 --> 00:46:03.300
and reinforce what are called in the novel

00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:07.233
the anti-Christian influences
which typify the age.

00:46:08.370 --> 00:46:10.377
Now not unlike Du Maurier's, Caine's,

00:46:10.377 --> 00:46:12.180
and Corelli's cryptic summaries,

00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:17.180
and as we've seen, these popular
novels could take all kinds

00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:20.910
of literary, cultural
stereotypes of the Jew

00:46:20.910 --> 00:46:23.910
and bring them all together.

00:46:23.910 --> 00:46:26.130
Thorne does exactly this with a vast array

00:46:26.130 --> 00:46:29.385
of Semitic representations
in a very short space.

00:46:29.385 --> 00:46:33.660
They begin with a red
haired, Victorian stage Jew.

00:46:33.660 --> 00:46:38.660
And Jews with red hair, actually,
up until the 19th century,

00:46:41.730 --> 00:46:43.350
even the end of the 19th century,

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:48.350
was much more common than having
the so-called Jewish nose.

00:46:52.500 --> 00:46:56.220
So red hair was very, very
common linked to the devil,

00:46:56.220 --> 00:46:58.213
of course, moving on chronologically

00:46:58.213 --> 00:47:01.560
to the hypnotical
controlling eyes of Svengali,

00:47:01.560 --> 00:47:04.470
and concluding with the
sinister Jew being categorized

00:47:04.470 --> 00:47:09.405
as a bestial, cold-hearted
intellectual, a reptile.

00:47:09.405 --> 00:47:11.730
Schuabe is finally defeated though,

00:47:11.730 --> 00:47:15.000
and this is the point,
by Sir Michael Manichoe,

00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:16.830
Sir Michael Manichoe,

00:47:16.830 --> 00:47:21.830
who is the benign mirror image of Schuabe.

00:47:22.200 --> 00:47:27.200
He's known as the pillar of Anglicanism,

00:47:27.270 --> 00:47:30.150
not Unitarianism, in the English church.

00:47:30.150 --> 00:47:34.920
So Michael Manichoe keeps
the English church going.

00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:38.520
And at the time that included Catholicism

00:47:38.520 --> 00:47:39.820
and the Catholic religion.

00:47:42.300 --> 00:47:46.890
We are told that Manichoe is
a man of great natural gifts,

00:47:46.890 --> 00:47:48.930
who has unimaginable wealth,

00:47:48.930 --> 00:47:51.360
is a Jewish convert to Christianity

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:53.880
and has also entered political life,

00:47:53.880 --> 00:47:56.130
quite literally a mirror image of Schuabe.

00:47:56.130 --> 00:47:59.787
And I'll read the slide below here.

00:47:59.787 --> 00:48:03.247
And we can see, Sir Michael
Manichoe, the quote below.

00:48:03.247 --> 00:48:05.820
"He's the owner of one of
those colossal Jewish fortunes,

00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:07.320
which, few as they are,

00:48:07.320 --> 00:48:10.410
have such a far-reaching
influence on English life.

00:48:10.410 --> 00:48:12.180
He employed it in such a way

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:16.530
which for a man in his
position was unique.

00:48:16.530 --> 00:48:19.740
He presented a curious
spectacle to sociologists

00:48:19.740 --> 00:48:21.150
and the world at large,

00:48:21.150 --> 00:48:24.090
of a Jew by origin who had
become a Christian by conviction

00:48:24.090 --> 00:48:27.330
and one of the sincerest
sons of the English church

00:48:27.330 --> 00:48:29.160
as he understood it.

00:48:29.160 --> 00:48:31.920
In political life Sir
Michael was a steady,

00:48:31.920 --> 00:48:35.973
rather than a brilliant force,
steady rather than brilliant.

00:48:38.485 --> 00:48:39.318
In English culture,

00:48:39.318 --> 00:48:41.793
you can't be too flashy,
you can't be too showy.

00:48:42.720 --> 00:48:45.330
So the other key thing to note here

00:48:45.330 --> 00:48:48.360
is that Sir Michael is given
no physical description

00:48:48.360 --> 00:48:51.630
to limit his uniquely benign presence

00:48:51.630 --> 00:48:54.030
in contrast to his malevolent double.

00:48:54.030 --> 00:48:56.940
And the Italian word for Manichoe

00:48:56.940 --> 00:49:00.450
means Manichaea in English.

00:49:00.450 --> 00:49:03.840
He's said to have a far-reaching
influence in English life,

00:49:03.840 --> 00:49:05.700
but only for good.

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:08.040
His political career is steady

00:49:08.040 --> 00:49:11.730
in contrast to the over-brained Schuabe.

00:49:11.730 --> 00:49:14.640
We don't like intellectuals.

00:49:14.640 --> 00:49:17.160
We don't like people to be over-brained.

00:49:17.160 --> 00:49:19.650
Who remains intellectually
detached from the country

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:21.600
which he's meant to serve.

00:49:21.600 --> 00:49:23.707
So Michael instead combines his quote,

00:49:23.707 --> 00:49:26.880
"Jewish brain and Christian heart,"

00:49:26.880 --> 00:49:29.190
Christian heart, which had, we're told,

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:33.720
already revolutionized
society nearly 2000 years ago,

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:35.670
2000 years ago.

00:49:35.670 --> 00:49:37.975
Such are the binaries
which structure the novel.

00:49:37.975 --> 00:49:42.975
One character personifies
Christ, the other, antichrist.

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:47.490
Only Sir Michael has the
so-called colossal Jewish fortune

00:49:47.490 --> 00:49:51.110
and Jewish brain to defeat
Schuabe, who is known

00:49:51.110 --> 00:49:54.660
as the most active of
God's enemies in England.

00:49:54.660 --> 00:49:56.733
And I've just got half a page to go.

00:49:57.870 --> 00:50:00.360
Caine's and Corelli's
fiction were also the subject

00:50:00.360 --> 00:50:03.030
of popular sermons,
theatrical productions,

00:50:03.030 --> 00:50:04.710
and many, many films.

00:50:04.710 --> 00:50:07.620
But in 1903, "The Daily Mail", rightly,

00:50:07.620 --> 00:50:10.800
a sensationalist newspaper then and now,

00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:12.570
rightly declared Thorne

00:50:12.570 --> 00:50:16.710
to be the most talked
about novelist in England.

00:50:16.710 --> 00:50:17.850
Not that the novel's claim

00:50:17.850 --> 00:50:21.750
to have generated a great
conspiracy is strictly accurate.

00:50:21.750 --> 00:50:24.570
The conspiracy in the
novel is easily contained

00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:29.340
and is ultimately the insane
folly of a single individual

00:50:29.340 --> 00:50:32.130
rather than a group of individuals.

00:50:32.130 --> 00:50:34.830
Thorne, to be sure, was
extravagant in his use

00:50:34.830 --> 00:50:39.150
of Semitic discourse, even
describing an elderly Jewish man

00:50:39.150 --> 00:50:43.110
as a great gray slug, which paved the way

00:50:43.110 --> 00:50:46.620
for cruder versions of
bestial, dehumanized Jews

00:50:46.620 --> 00:50:48.570
in the decades following.

00:50:48.570 --> 00:50:51.000
But "When It Was Dark",
following "Trilby",

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:55.590
is a good deal more ambivalent
than its one-eyed reception.

00:50:55.590 --> 00:50:57.330
The reader could choose
between the good Jew

00:50:57.330 --> 00:50:59.455
of liberal virtue, or
the bad Jew who needs

00:50:59.455 --> 00:51:03.210
to be banished from the national sphere,

00:51:03.210 --> 00:51:05.820
which happens at the end of the novel.

00:51:05.820 --> 00:51:08.910
Such binaries distinguishing
between light and dark

00:51:08.910 --> 00:51:12.960
characterized everyday
antisemitism in a liberal context

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:16.080
and allowed those who engage
in race thinking about Jews

00:51:16.080 --> 00:51:18.505
to deny that they were antisemitic,

00:51:18.505 --> 00:51:21.120
something that happens to this day.

00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:22.380
The liberal antisemitism

00:51:22.380 --> 00:51:26.160
or the antisemitism of
tolerance as it's been called

00:51:26.160 --> 00:51:28.120
continues to this day.

00:51:28.120 --> 00:51:32.283
And I have written about it
very much in contemporary terms.

00:51:33.990 --> 00:51:38.913
So to finish just a concluding
slide, if there's time.

00:51:40.166 --> 00:51:43.470
Just very quickly, but we
can go over all of this

00:51:43.470 --> 00:51:45.660
in the question and answers.

00:51:45.660 --> 00:51:49.170
I'm talking here about
mass produced literature.

00:51:49.170 --> 00:51:51.770
I'm asking the question,
was Marshall McLuhan right?

00:51:52.770 --> 00:51:55.110
So is the medium the message?

00:51:55.110 --> 00:51:57.540
I'm saying mass produced literature

00:51:57.540 --> 00:52:00.540
mainstreams the extreme, absolutely.

00:52:00.540 --> 00:52:02.220
And is a new medium,

00:52:02.220 --> 00:52:05.250
but does not have a single message.

00:52:05.250 --> 00:52:07.743
Even bestsellers are
complicated narratives.

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:12.630
I distinguish between liberal bifurcation,

00:52:12.630 --> 00:52:17.630
liberal bifurcation,
which is something that's

00:52:18.390 --> 00:52:20.760
between desirable and undesirable Jews,

00:52:20.760 --> 00:52:23.790
which has very much become state policy.

00:52:23.790 --> 00:52:28.790
So I'm saying this happens
at an institutional level.

00:52:29.850 --> 00:52:32.430
It produces race thinking,

00:52:32.430 --> 00:52:35.403
but is not the same as
illiberal exclusion.

00:52:37.085 --> 00:52:41.193
But both, as we saw at
the beginning, coexist.

00:52:42.120 --> 00:52:43.800
I also talk about religious, racial,

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:47.100
and nationalist discourses
all being intertwined.

00:52:47.100 --> 00:52:50.820
So the linear narrative here
that looks at one at a time

00:52:50.820 --> 00:52:53.010
I think is mistaken.

00:52:53.010 --> 00:52:57.015
And finally, the key
thing that we must not do

00:52:57.015 --> 00:53:02.015
is read teleologically, or to back shadow,

00:53:04.830 --> 00:53:09.090
to read First World War
text as leading inevitably

00:53:09.090 --> 00:53:13.113
either to the Protocols
or to antisemitic hatred.

00:53:14.010 --> 00:53:15.303
And there I finish.

00:53:17.130 --> 00:53:18.510
Thank you so much.

00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:20.460
- Yeah, thank you Bryan.

00:53:20.460 --> 00:53:21.630
That was so interesting.

00:53:21.630 --> 00:53:23.340
And I have a bunch of questions,

00:53:23.340 --> 00:53:25.200
and I ask people to submit them

00:53:25.200 --> 00:53:26.880
in the question and answer period,

00:53:26.880 --> 00:53:28.380
I mean question and answer function

00:53:28.380 --> 00:53:31.080
at the bottom of your screen.

00:53:31.080 --> 00:53:33.180
We don't have a lot of time,
but maybe we'll go like,

00:53:33.180 --> 00:53:37.830
five minutes over just
to be able to talk to you

00:53:37.830 --> 00:53:39.390
a little bit.

00:53:39.390 --> 00:53:43.440
So first I'll take you up on your offer to

00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:46.020
talk a little bit more about your concept

00:53:46.020 --> 00:53:49.717
of Semitic discourse, which
is really your, I feel like,

00:53:49.717 --> 00:53:52.890
you know, a major
contribution that you've made

00:53:52.890 --> 00:53:56.755
to understanding this idea

00:53:56.755 --> 00:54:01.755
that a lot of the discourse
around the Jew is racializing,

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:05.130
but is not necessarily negative

00:54:05.130 --> 00:54:06.840
and not necessarily positive,

00:54:06.840 --> 00:54:09.450
but is sort of in this in-between area.

00:54:09.450 --> 00:54:13.020
Can you talk a little bit
more about what that concept

00:54:13.020 --> 00:54:15.660
allows you to get at?

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:16.493
- Yes.

00:54:17.850 --> 00:54:22.740
I think talking about ambivalence
or ambiguity is important

00:54:22.740 --> 00:54:23.910
and important to this day

00:54:23.910 --> 00:54:26.580
and important even on social media.

00:54:26.580 --> 00:54:29.880
So I think quite often
people just simply talk about

00:54:29.880 --> 00:54:32.100
hatred or hostility.

00:54:32.100 --> 00:54:34.105
Now even going back to
Christianity, as I've shown,

00:54:34.105 --> 00:54:37.860
and to the Christian
foundations of anti Judaism,

00:54:37.860 --> 00:54:42.330
that was equally bifurcated.

00:54:42.330 --> 00:54:45.810
So Jews who converted
were seen as a good thing,

00:54:45.810 --> 00:54:50.220
as well as being seen as
Christ killers, antichrist.

00:54:50.220 --> 00:54:53.040
So that bifurcation goes
very much back to the roots

00:54:53.040 --> 00:54:56.010
of Christianity, and I
think very much feeds

00:54:56.010 --> 00:55:00.513
into liberal culture, western
liberal democratic culture.

00:55:01.920 --> 00:55:05.280
Obviously once you get a
more authoritarian culture,

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:08.850
Nazism, fascism, Stalinism,
then it's a different story.

00:55:08.850 --> 00:55:12.690
But I'm talking here about
western liberal culture.

00:55:12.690 --> 00:55:13.980
And I think the problem

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:18.360
with looking at anti-Semitism
just as a form of hatred

00:55:18.360 --> 00:55:20.100
is that it answers its own question.

00:55:20.100 --> 00:55:23.310
That's all that's ever
looked at in social media.

00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:26.370
So other kinds of images of Jews

00:55:26.370 --> 00:55:29.490
and the good-bad discourse
is simply ignored,

00:55:29.490 --> 00:55:32.037
which seems to me, and
I've looked at social media

00:55:32.037 --> 00:55:34.800
and the whole of Labor Party controversy

00:55:34.800 --> 00:55:38.010
was about dividing Jews up into those

00:55:38.010 --> 00:55:39.410
that were socialist and not.

00:55:41.250 --> 00:55:43.170
So to me it's essential,

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:48.170
but for all kinds of reasons
is not followed through.

00:55:48.581 --> 00:55:50.518
And I can make the link
with gender as well.

00:55:50.518 --> 00:55:53.640
It's a bit like thinking
about gender only in terms

00:55:53.640 --> 00:55:55.470
of misogyny and nothing else.

00:55:55.470 --> 00:55:57.420
- Yeah, and that's something
that I've worked on

00:55:57.420 --> 00:56:00.900
a little bit too, that often that split

00:56:00.900 --> 00:56:02.970
is also a gendered split.

00:56:02.970 --> 00:56:04.950
So especially in the French context,

00:56:04.950 --> 00:56:06.780
which I've looked at in the 19th century,

00:56:06.780 --> 00:56:10.650
Jewish women are idealized, sexualized

00:56:10.650 --> 00:56:12.450
and Jewish men are demonized.

00:56:12.450 --> 00:56:14.430
And that, you know, becomes two sides--

00:56:14.430 --> 00:56:17.730
- Absolutely, because Jewish
women can convert, right?

00:56:17.730 --> 00:56:19.107
They're seen as--

00:56:19.107 --> 00:56:21.930
- And their body isn't
marked by Jewishness.

00:56:21.930 --> 00:56:22.763
- Exactly.

00:56:22.763 --> 00:56:24.780
And they're seen, especially
young Jewish women,

00:56:24.780 --> 00:56:27.120
it's also ageist as well.

00:56:27.120 --> 00:56:29.320
But yes, only Marie Corelli

00:56:30.810 --> 00:56:35.385
can take the desirable
Jewish woman, so-called,

00:56:35.385 --> 00:56:37.050
and turn her into something else.

00:56:37.050 --> 00:56:39.903
- I apologize for my cold, my sniffling.

00:56:40.950 --> 00:56:43.978
- I don't mind, I'm here. (laughing)

00:56:43.978 --> 00:56:46.380
- Another question I
have is to what extent

00:56:46.380 --> 00:56:51.380
this is a British story
here that you're telling,

00:56:51.510 --> 00:56:53.700
or an Anglo-American one.

00:56:53.700 --> 00:56:57.960
Versus, you know, let's say
the French or the German case.

00:56:57.960 --> 00:57:02.550
So is some of this, you know,

00:57:02.550 --> 00:57:06.480
ambiguity particularly
English in the 19th century?

00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:08.777
- No, I really don't think so.

00:57:13.228 --> 00:57:18.228
I think it's much more to do with the form

00:57:19.680 --> 00:57:22.170
that the western democratic states took.

00:57:22.170 --> 00:57:24.780
And in Germany famously,

00:57:24.780 --> 00:57:27.267
you know, the Ostjuden was demonized,

00:57:30.776 --> 00:57:33.030
accultured German Jews weren't.

00:57:33.030 --> 00:57:35.820
And I think that split, in France as well.

00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:37.427
I mean, you know, as long as you read--

00:57:37.427 --> 00:57:40.950
- In France, it's often
mapped onto the difference,

00:57:40.950 --> 00:57:42.990
I mean, one of the things
that defines the French case

00:57:42.990 --> 00:57:44.640
is the mix between the Sephardic

00:57:44.640 --> 00:57:46.515
and the Ashkenazi populations,

00:57:46.515 --> 00:57:50.430
and that the Sephardic
Jews tend to be idealized

00:57:50.430 --> 00:57:52.350
and the Ashkenazi Jews demonized.

00:57:52.350 --> 00:57:53.370
- Absolutely.

00:57:53.370 --> 00:57:56.170
I mean, I think there's all
kinds of ways of doing this.

00:57:57.600 --> 00:57:59.250
And it does vary from national culture

00:57:59.250 --> 00:58:01.683
to national culture, no question.

00:58:03.034 --> 00:58:03.867
And because France

00:58:03.867 --> 00:58:08.867
has a much more ideological
version of this, but even so,

00:58:08.910 --> 00:58:12.600
I think the key is western European.

00:58:12.600 --> 00:58:14.750
It's different, say,
in Russian literature.

00:58:16.500 --> 00:58:17.460
Yeah.

00:58:17.460 --> 00:58:19.920
- Okay, maybe last question.

00:58:19.920 --> 00:58:24.570
This is all playing out against
the backdrop of the rise

00:58:24.570 --> 00:58:26.310
of the Zionist movement,

00:58:26.310 --> 00:58:29.370
and that's something that I
feel like in the Hall Caine,

00:58:29.370 --> 00:58:31.560
it sort of came up a little bit.

00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:34.380
But if you could just say
a little bit more about

00:58:34.380 --> 00:58:39.380
how the Zionist movement, you
know, enters into this story.

00:58:40.740 --> 00:58:43.830
- Well, I think certainly
the Zionist movement,

00:58:43.830 --> 00:58:47.160
and I talk about this in "Constructions"

00:58:47.160 --> 00:58:50.130
right at the beginning,
and in other articles.

00:58:50.130 --> 00:58:51.120
I've written quite a lot

00:58:51.120 --> 00:58:53.283
on antisemitism and post-colonialism.

00:58:55.050 --> 00:58:57.990
But also divided Jews
up into good and bad.

00:58:57.990 --> 00:59:00.210
I mean, just in a different way.

00:59:00.210 --> 00:59:05.210
So Jews were seen as a good
thing if they thought that,

00:59:06.900 --> 00:59:08.640
believed in a form of nationalism,

00:59:08.640 --> 00:59:10.620
especially from the Zionist point of view,

00:59:10.620 --> 00:59:12.840
Palestinian-centered nationalism

00:59:12.840 --> 00:59:15.543
as opposed to Zangwill's
Jewish territorialism.

00:59:18.600 --> 00:59:20.790
And did denigrate the diaspora.

00:59:20.790 --> 00:59:24.340
You know, Herzl famously talked
about the ghetto mentality

00:59:25.591 --> 00:59:29.160
and the diaspora being somehow degenerate.

00:59:29.160 --> 00:59:32.730
And Max Nordau of course
was a number two to Herzl,

00:59:32.730 --> 00:59:35.490
and wrote a wonderful book on degeneracy.

00:59:35.490 --> 00:59:40.490
So I think that the
bifurcation of Jewish binaries

00:59:41.250 --> 00:59:43.560
apply to Zionism.

00:59:43.560 --> 00:59:45.330
Also to anti-Semites as well.

00:59:45.330 --> 00:59:48.250
So you could be anti-Semitic
and think it was great

00:59:49.758 --> 00:59:54.355
in "Daniel Deronda" that
Jews would leave Europe.

00:59:54.355 --> 00:59:56.830
- Okay, last question, I'm just gonna take

00:59:57.990 --> 01:00:00.150
just the advantage to go
a little bit over time

01:00:00.150 --> 01:00:05.150
and just ask you, are there Jewish reviews

01:00:05.340 --> 01:00:06.420
of these novels?

01:00:06.420 --> 01:00:08.910
Like in "The Jewish
Chronicle" for example,

01:00:08.910 --> 01:00:09.990
are there reviews?

01:00:09.990 --> 01:00:12.750
Like, how were Jews reacting?

01:00:12.750 --> 01:00:15.690
Did Zangwill himself
like, respond to some?

01:00:15.690 --> 01:00:17.767
- Yeah, Zangwill did,

01:00:17.767 --> 01:00:19.890
and Zangwill was in the
audience when Hall Caine spoke

01:00:19.890 --> 01:00:21.577
at the Maccabeans,

01:00:21.577 --> 01:00:24.063
"The Jewish Chronicle"
reviewed Hall Caine.

01:00:26.640 --> 01:00:28.980
And they were quite sensitive

01:00:28.980 --> 01:00:31.890
towards straightforwardly
anti-Semitic works,

01:00:31.890 --> 01:00:35.340
so Marie Corelli and others,

01:00:35.340 --> 01:00:38.310
but didn't pick up on the subtleties.

01:00:38.310 --> 01:00:39.840
And didn't pick up on the fact

01:00:39.840 --> 01:00:41.730
that Hall Caine also divided Jews up

01:00:41.730 --> 01:00:43.800
into desirable and undesirable

